{"id": "3b19ed0b-0868-4499-b5b5-0f1e9995a1fb", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll dig into this here. I'm starting a deep pass on Bedrock usage: output tokens against request volume per model, which callers or applications are driving it, and what changed when it started climbing.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_ZdY6oFw9vAuNZq7ixHyzrv", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "bedrock-output-token-deep-dive", "agent_type": "generic", "preloaded_skills": ["exploring-metrics", "searching-logs", "investigating-infrastructure-changes", "discovering-deployments", "exploring-code", "exploring-traces", "discovering-topology", "verifying-with-docs"], "prompt": "[DEEP RESEARCH MODE]
{
  "task": "Find out where Bedrock output-token spend is going and why it keeps climbing while request volume is flat. Identify the specific driver(s): which model(s), which callers/applications/IAM roles/inference profiles, which features, and what changed and when.",
  "context": "User reports the Amazon Bedrock bill is dominated by OUTPUT tokens and has been steadily increasing even though request (invocation) volume is flat. That means output tokens per request is rising, or traffic is shifting to a pricier model. They want a root-cause-style answer in chat. Agent space account is 123456789012, region us-east-1, but also check other associated AWS accounts (use list_associations) and any other regions where Bedrock is used (e.g., us-west-2, eu-west-1) since the usage may live there.",
  "resources": ["AWS/Bedrock CloudWatch namespace (Invocations, InputTokenCount, OutputTokenCount, InvocationLatency, InvocationClientErrors/ServerErrors/Throttles) by ModelId", "Bedrock model invocation logging destinations (CloudWatch Logs log group and/or S3 bucket) - check get_model_invocation_logging_configuration", "Bedrock inference profiles, provisioned throughput, Agents, Knowledge Bases, Guardrails", "Lambda/ECS/EKS workloads that call bedrock-runtime", "Cost Explorer (ce get_cost_and_usage grouped by USAGE_TYPE / LINKED_ACCOUNT, service Amazon Bedrock) if permitted"],
  "timeframe": "Last 60 days up to 2026-11-20T04:00:00Z. Use daily granularity to find the inflection point, compare a baseline window against the recent window.",
  "criteria": "1) Quantify trend: daily Invocations vs OutputTokenCount vs InputTokenCount, and the derived output-tokens-per-invocation ratio, per ModelId; find the date the slope changed. 2) Check for model mix shift (e.g., move to a larger or more expensive model, or new model IDs/inference profiles appearing). 3) If invocation logging is enabled, analyze logs to break down output tokens by caller identity/IAM role, modelId, requestMetadata, and max_tokens settings; look for outliers like very long completions, runaway generation hitting max_tokens, retries/loops, agentic or tool-use loops, verbose reasoning/extended thinking being enabled, prompt changes that ask for longer outputs, or streaming calls that get retried. 4) Check Bedrock Agents / Knowledge Bases (RetrieveAndGenerate) / Guardrails as hidden multipliers. 5) Correlate the inflection date with deployments, code changes (look for max_tokens, temperature, system prompt, thinking/reasoning budget changes in repositories if accessible), config changes, and CloudTrail events (e.g., new models enabled, new inference profiles, logging changes). 6) Cost Explorer usage-type breakdown for output-token cost by model if available. If invocation logging is NOT enabled, say so clearly and report what can be determined from metrics alone and what to enable. Report concrete numbers, dates and identifiers, state your confidence in each hypothesis, say what was ruled out, and list recommended concrete next steps to reduce output tokens (e.g., cap max_tokens, add stop sequences, prompt tuning for concision, cheaper model for specific workloads, caching/batch)."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Deep dive into Bedrock output token usage trends and drivers"}], "created_at": null}

